<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mennonites</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mennonites are Anabaptist Christians named after Menno Simons, a Frisian former priest who organised scattered Anabaptist groups in the 1530s and 1540s. Rejecting infant baptism, oaths and military service, they suffered persecution and settled wherever rulers tolerated them, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, small German territories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mennonites.jpg" alt="Mennonites" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mennonite World Conference · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mennonite_World_Conference_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mennonites trace their roots to the Radical Reformation in the German and Dutch-speaking regions of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Their persecutors called the forerunners of the movement Täufer or Wiedertäufer, re-baptizers. Their most distinguishing feature was the rejection of infant baptism, an act with political as well as religious meaning, since almost every infant born in western Europe was baptized in the Roman Catholic Church. Their views also developed in opposition to those of Protestant reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>.</p><p>Menno Simons, a Frisian cleric who lived from 1496 to 1561, became a prominent leader of the wider movement and formalized the teachings of earlier Swiss Anabaptist founders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and North German background took their name from him, and the Swiss and South German group later adopted it too. A third group, organized by Jakob Hutter, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 16th century the Mennonites and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> were relentlessly persecuted. Martyrs Mirror, published in 1660, records much of it, including accounts of over 4,000 burnings, and numerous stonings, imprisonments and live burials. Persecution continued in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> until 1710. Rather than resist by arms, most early followers survived by fleeing to neighbouring states whose ruling families tolerated believer's baptism.</p><p>Beliefs were set out in the Schleitheim Confession of 24 February 1527, which covered the ban, communion, separation from the world, believer's baptism, pastors, renunciation of the sword and renunciation of the oath. The Dordrecht Confession of Faith, adopted by Dutch Mennonites on 21 April 1632, was later taken up by Alsatian and North American Mennonites.</p><p>Persecution and the search for employment drove Mennonites from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> eastward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the 17th century. Others settled around Danzig and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a> from about 1530, and from 1791 they founded colonies in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. In 1683 families from Krefeld settled at Germantown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, the first permanent Mennonite settlement in the American colonies, and in 1688 four of them signed the first formal protest against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Mennonites are known as one of the historic peace churches because of their pacifism. Congregations range from Old Order Mennonites, who live without certain elements of modern technology, to mainline Mennonites who dress like the general population. There is no single world authority, and the Mennonite World Conference, founded at Basel in 1925, coordinates shared causes without making binding decisions. The Mennonite Central Committee, founded in Chicago on 27 September 1920, provides disaster relief and development work around the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mennonites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch Reformed Church was the public church, but Catholics, <b>Mennonites</b>, Lutherans and Jews could worship privately, and Sephardic Jews from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Anabaptist leaders such as Menno Simons, a former priest from Friesland, gathered communities that survived persecution, later named <b>Mennonites</b> after him.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Fiercely persecuted, their movement gave rise to the <b>Mennonites</b>, Amish and Hutterites.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Neuwied welcomed <b>Mennonites</b>, Moravian Brethren, Catholics, Jews and Reformed Christians, each with its own congregation.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wied-neuwied">County of Wied-Neuwied</a></li></ul></section>
